ARM R — broader-capability / sticky-reopen SAFETY arm for lever N (plan_autoadvance, ADR-0168)
Pre-registered 2026-09-13 09:36 UTC, BEFORE launch. Branch arm-r-capability-27b.
Build: origin/main 5dfbf14 (post-#434 fix). Bench src on zc-01 verified byte-identical to origin/main
(tasks.py 657b87bb, loop.py f76b12ff) and both #434 fixes present: fix(a) prior_by_title keyed on
(title, is-parent); fix(b) doom-guard last_autoadvanced reset. (Letters O and Q skipped in THIS lever-N
series M->N->P->R: O unused; "ARM Q" already names the temp-unset arm in determinism-partition.log,
unrelated to lever N.)

WHY THIS ARM. Lever N is already proven to (a) BREAK the 35B doom loop in situ (arm-P diagnostic: 2/6
--no-pin runs entered the resend pattern, adv=3 and adv=4, and BOTH walked to a complete+verified finish,
0 doom, vs ~80% doom among triggered runs on the unfixed build) and (b) be BYTE-IDENTICALLY INERT when the
model does not trigger it (arm-P P4: OFF==ON output digests on all 10 non-triggering pinned runs). The ONE
untested downside gating a default-on flip is the STICKY-REOPEN RISK: lever N marks a worked step done and
makes it sticky (Task.harness_done survives replace_from_author's full-replace), so a model that resends
the plan with that step back to pending CANNOT reopen it. On the 35B that is the whole point (it will not
emit status:done, so the harness must do it). On a MORE CAPABLE / better-behaved model that DOES tick
status and might LEGITIMATELY reopen a step, the same stickiness could be a BUG — a deterministic harness
advance overriding a correct model decision. This arm measures whether that harm is real.

HONEST CAVEAT (measured 2026-09-13, models_probe.py against the pod /v1/models). zakpod1 serves EXACTLY
THREE models, NONE larger than 35B: zds-qwen3.5-35b, zds-qwen3.6-35b (the doom-loop subject),
zds-qwen3.8-27b. There is NO >35B / frontier model testable here. The best-available proxy for "a more
capable, better-behaved model" is zds-qwen3.8-27b: the NEWEST minor version, and the model arm J/K measured
completing task 10 in fewer turns (27B 15->8-11 turns; the 35B went 3/3->1/3 under the survey). It is
SMALLER by parameter count but NEWER and better-behaved on this task. So this arm tests "does lever N harm
a BETTER-BEHAVED model", NOT "a BIGGER model"; the >35B regime stays UNMEASURED and is a documented residual
on any default-on decision. Mitigant already shipped: lever N has an OPT-OUT (leave the flag unset), so a
big-model user harmed by a future default-on can disable it.

MODEL: zds-qwen3.8-27b (ZBENCH_POD_MODEL). TASK: 10-rule-in-pyproject (the doom-loop task). temperature 0,
ZAKCODE_STABLE_PROMPT_IDENTITY=1, survey ON (default), verify_auto OFF. Both arms share the ONE verified
build; only ZAKCODE_PLAN_AUTOADVANCE differs (OFF = unset, the shipped default; ON = 1). Runner
/root/zb-armr.sh; scorer /root/armr-summary.py; dumps /root/zb-dumps/R-* (NEVER committed; instruments
print counts/hashes only); result JSONs
bench/results/determinism-zakcode-pin{1,OFF}-temp0-10-rule-in-pyproject.R-*.json.

CELLS.
  Cell 1 — PINNED pair (byte-identical control + same-basin Q4 test). default pin (ZBENCH_PIN_MODE=1:
    constant workspace AND constant session id 000...0 -- the STRICT byte-identical basin arm-P P4 used,
    evidenced by its pin1 result files), N=3 each {OFF, ON}, interleaved OFF b1 / ON b1 / OFF b2 / ON b2 /
    OFF b3 / ON b3. Same basin every run at temp 0 -> near-deterministic. If the 27B does NOT trigger ->
    R1 (byte-identical inertness for the 27B). If it DOES -> R3 direct (same basin, ON advance vs OFF
    no-advance, verify parity).
  Cell 2 — --no-pin sample (basin diversity, rb-10849). N=6 each {OFF, ON} as two interleaved blocks of three: OFF b1, ON b1, OFF b2, ON b2, so any
    time-drift in the basin distribution (rb-10849 non-stationarity) hits both arms roughly equally.
    Fresh mkdtemp workspace per run. Gives the 27B trigger RATE (R4) and the DISTRIBUTIONAL outcome parity
    ON vs OFF (R2, doom rate).

MEASURE THE TRIGGER FIRST. Before scoring any rule, report per cell: n; triggered =
count(trace_interventions.plan_autoadvance > 0); the adv values; doom = count(stop_reason==doom_loop);
completed count; pass = count(verify_rc==0); turn list. A rule scored without its trigger count is VOID
(guard: a trigger of zero routes the verdict to the diagnostic, never to a silent pass).

RULES + PREDICTIONS (measured cause to be stated beside each at results time).
  R1 (Q2 -- inertness holds for the 27B). PREDICT: on every Cell-1 run where the 27B does NOT trigger
     (plan_autoadvance==0), ON and OFF produce byte-identical output digests (digests/py_digests/sources).
     PASS = all non-triggering pinned runs byte-identical ON vs OFF. [Inertness is a property of the CODE,
     not of the model, so it should hold for any model; confirming on the 27B makes the arm self-contained.]
  R2 (Q3 -- no distributional outcome regression). PREDICT: in Cell 2, ON pass rate >= OFF pass rate AND
     ON doom rate <= OFF doom rate. A better-behaved model that triggers less should show ON ~= OFF (both
     good), or ON slightly better (lever N rescuing the rare stuck run). PASS = ON not worse than OFF on
     pass rate AND on doom rate.
  R3 (Q4 -- no same-basin reopen HARM, the load-bearing safety rule). PREDICT: zero runs where ON triggers
     and yields verify_rc==1 on a basin where OFF yields verify_rc==0. Cleanest evidence: any Cell-1 pinned
     run where the 27B triggers and ON's verify_rc > OFF's verify_rc. Secondary: a post-hoc dump scan of
     ON-triggered runs for a model reopen-ATTEMPT (a plan step sent done->pending AFTER a harness advance)
     that ON blocked AND that cost the outcome. PASS = no ON-worse-than-OFF verify on a shared basin AND no
     outcome-costing reopen-attempt.
  R4 (Q1 -- trigger rate, DESCRIPTIVE not a gate). PREDICT: the 27B triggers lever N at a LOWER rate than
     the 35B's rate on this task/basin family (a better-behaved model ticks status:done more reliably ->
     the plan changes -> fewer byte-unchanged resends -> lever N rarely arms). Per "detection outranks
     attribution", the SAFETY rules R2/R3 gate the flip; R4 only informs understanding.

VERDICT RULE (default-on flip). IF R1 (inert-when-idle) AND R2 (no distributional regression) AND R3 (no
same-basin reopen harm) ALL hold -> FLIP lever N default-on, documenting the >35B residual and the opt-out.
IF R2 OR R3 FAILS -> keep lever N OPT-IN and record the measured downside (the sticky-reopen harm made
concrete). R4 informs understanding either way.
